  "result": "All four tests pass.\n\nI implemented `catalog/service.py` with the `NotFound` exception and the `Catalog` class. Records live in an insertion-ordered dict keyed by id, so listing in creation order falls out for free. Update validates field names before touching the record and rejects anything other than title or author with a `ValueError`. Callers get copies of records, so mutating a returned dict cannot corrupt the catalog.\n\nI chose Approach A, sequential integers, because the catalog is in-memory and per-instance, so there is no cross-process coordination that would justify UUIDs. A private counter gives short, sortable, deterministic ids that are easy to read in tests and logs, and since the counter never reuses values after a delete, ids remain unique and stable for a record's lifetime.",
